#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f806240-4d0c-5c85-b338-51b52df8ece9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Additional Linear Term"}] :question/id #uuid "4e5af199-faf5-540b-ad83-c3090e761183" :question/math-academy-id "e-21230" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+10} & =x-2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{x+10})}^{2} & ={(x-2)}^{2} \\\\ x+10 & ={x}^{2}-4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-1+10} & \\overset{?}{=}-1-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{9} & \\overset{?}{=}-3 \\\\ 3 & \\ne -3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=6$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6+10} & \\overset{?}{=}6-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=6$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2$  is $x=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054563 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"}] :question/id #uuid "18c11d40-eed5-5af6-ade4-70e024af6cd1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5232" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2x-6} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{2x-6})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 2x-6 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(5)-6} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2$  is $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054564 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "1d21fa8f-95d6-55b7-916f-b12d00f2f002" :question/math-academy-id "e-5233" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{5-x} = -2$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{4-x} = 5$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{2x+1} = -1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054565 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/id #uuid "2c646210-4559-5e42-9feb-05428d1f674c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5234" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8})}^{2} & ={(2)}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+8 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x=4,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-5(1)+8}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-5(4)+8}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=1$ and $x=4$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f55c074-5c1b-568c-a7ea-28e935a77130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532029} {:answer/id #uuid "39f74041-ca9c-5dc0-ae3b-06c900366b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532052} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2af395-ee42-5de0-838f-0e24f21ad488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532217} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e35b7c-8732-531f-9d1d-833f24e8eca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532364} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6ac876-47f6-5460-97e7-ff997a6d48df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd6ac876-47f6-5460-97e7-ff997a6d48df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532489} :answer-field/id #uuid "f90f12a0-ed27-5b63-9d08-dbfcadce5512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e628d1de-d244-5147-9b9f-c225b0748668" :question/math-academy-id "q-114606" :question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{7-2x}=-2$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{x-3}=-7$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{6x-2}=5$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and II state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and II have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce5edb2-e927-5ea3-b67d-0bf3cc56ffbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531958} {:answer/id #uuid "5a03a6f4-3c36-552e-83ef-902feff74a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532131} {:answer/id #uuid "7832e3c6-f6f7-52f5-be46-35293bf9931e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532201} {:answer/id #uuid "9a95c02f-bd33-5a18-a681-603645cf3866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532286} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0cb403-5d52-5f25-8a18-82eb266418a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d0cb403-5d52-5f25-8a18-82eb266418a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532291} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1955fe9-f651-5dc5-b349-16fe6f39ca4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "028210c4-ea68-52ea-a069-08f10f6556b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-3037" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\sqrt{x-4}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x-4} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{x-4})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x-4 & =4 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8-4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{x-4}=2$  is $x=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f806240-4d0c-5c85-b338-51b52df8ece9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Additional Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "368da266-8452-530b-a457-5e1f941fd39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532043} {:answer/id #uuid "4d30bd5a-8805-5386-be83-277816027d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532099} {:answer/id #uuid "52881457-4fd5-53db-a157-640a3789bbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532111} {:answer/id #uuid "73e46bfd-7668-5274-b080-fd60ce6d36c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532196} {:answer/id #uuid "85d3e197-b880-50b5-b5ba-28ae572ba36f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52881457-4fd5-53db-a157-640a3789bbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532111} :answer-field/id #uuid "46723694-ff57-5a32-a595-df0dbc65a0fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7893b3a-653a-5126-aece-84c455846266" :question/math-academy-id "q-39659" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ if given that $\\sqrt{14-x}=x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{14-x}=x-2$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14-x} & =x-2 \\\\ (\\sqrt{14-x})^{2} & =(x-2)^{2} \\\\ 14-x & ={x}^{2}-4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+x-14 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+2x-10 & =0 \\\\ x(x-5)+2(x-5) & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =5,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=5$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14-5} & \\overset{?}{=}5-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{9} & \\overset{?}{=}5-2 \\\\ 3 & \\overset{?}{=}5-2 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=5$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=-2$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14-(-2)} & \\overset{?}{=}-2-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}-2-2 \\\\ 4 & \\ne -4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $\\sqrt{14-x}=x-2$  is $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f806240-4d0c-5c85-b338-51b52df8ece9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Additional Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f8d68d-f40d-5c24-a4ce-7305f5d2d6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531948} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb86604-5a0d-5a3a-bf6d-b040bfda5b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4,\\,36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531996} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4dd67f-9f7f-54c7-b37f-0fff84e54ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532087} {:answer/id #uuid "8e61b4e8-7863-5d7c-bc6f-47883ba042b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532255} {:answer/id #uuid "da845571-4594-594d-9eae-214c174f9f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da845571-4594-594d-9eae-214c174f9f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1,\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532403} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbf90299-3d38-5a19-b9b4-8be38acea213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cfcdd38-dccd-5180-beb8-71439df888fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-39756" :question/problem "Solve $2-3\\sqrt{x}=-x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the equation $2-3\\sqrt{x}=-x,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\sqrt{x} & =-x \\\\ -3\\sqrt{x} & =-x-2 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{x} & =x+2 \\\\ {(3\\sqrt{x})}^{2} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ 9x & ={x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+(-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)-4(x-1) & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ 2-3 & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ -1 & =-1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=1$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=4$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-4 \\\\ 2-6 & \\overset{?}{=}-4 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=4$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $2-3\\sqrt{x}=-x$  are $x=1,\\,4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5837311-e37d-57ff-a9c1-422d5569df08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d80fc2b-952d-5d97-b472-408f474f4301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052759} {:answer/id #uuid "21df286c-eaee-5596-9ba1-d3247025a1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532003} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2a6f70-7ba2-51f5-8315-7c292c85e4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532017} {:answer/id #uuid "732cb569-f017-5ff2-b071-6b7823f7731f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532193} {:answer/id #uuid "f88399db-f363-5198-a216-2e06441b4d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d80fc2b-952d-5d97-b472-408f474f4301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052759} :answer-field/id #uuid "f36c2096-a42c-5241-9914-d9b637a3c9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fceba246-1aa7-568a-aca1-de613dbe563a" :question/math-academy-id "q-39822" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{1-7x}=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nTherefore, the given equation has no real solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1c429c4-17f9-5874-952f-2ae118e180d3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4a8afe-8653-50fc-a6c2-079485b7e081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531957} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5b79ee-698b-5f26-8367-9f89b495e958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532088} {:answer/id #uuid "53cdd14f-96d5-58b5-8397-9f6903783339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532115} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3a60c2-5b06-56d5-a3cc-c730cf971d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532293} {:answer/id #uuid "e6babe52-b3d1-5761-b735-56416c05cc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53cdd14f-96d5-58b5-8397-9f6903783339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532115} :answer-field/id #uuid "11ab1b23-6e87-5f18-bf90-319aa4e40494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cd9e82e-52f1-5f56-a2b7-86d371d48c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-39826" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{3x+4}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3x+4} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{3x+4})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 3x+4 & =16 \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3\\cdot 4+4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{12+4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{3x+4}=4$  is $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2630618c-0efc-5db4-8afb-2f86f8998beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532011} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea44428-82fd-5d84-8e60-4fa4d6580387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532027} {:answer/id #uuid "4db5a159-03f8-5808-8203-a4b6895bbc3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532101} {:answer/id #uuid "a06c23de-53b7-5f6e-a082-ea88dc045b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532295} {:answer/id #uuid "d6676e87-a4bb-50ce-84b9-6f657fa4982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a06c23de-53b7-5f6e-a082-ea88dc045b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532295} :answer-field/id #uuid "f98d1a0e-e08a-51f4-8f73-306cde22d069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28c9adff-f6cc-5086-b62e-6ff879b21f10" :question/math-academy-id "q-39830" :question/problem "Solve $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the term $-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}$ by subtracting $8$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $-6$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2} & =12 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} & ={12}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =144 \\\\ x & =142.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{142+2}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{\\sqrt{144}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{12}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=142$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6$  is $x=142.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90cf7ef5-e6f8-59ba-bac9-68370011b547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no real solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054058} {:answer/id #uuid "07803253-951f-58de-951c-3a4c24cb8662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531949} {:answer/id #uuid "136fd48c-2cb3-5fa9-9b7b-625e2dc24ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531971} {:answer/id #uuid "699690f3-59a4-585a-b6de-f37f5b8099bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532170} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6bbfc2-4600-5b1d-a17c-9006b8303d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea6bbfc2-4600-5b1d-a17c-9006b8303d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532438} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd2c8fe6-ab40-5aa6-807d-8e4d83bc02e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a775f77a-5da2-50d5-b383-16e892933964" :question/math-academy-id "q-70448" :question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3}-3=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root by adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3} & =3 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3})}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+3 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-(-2)+3}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+2+3}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{9}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 3-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-3+3}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{9-3+3}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{9}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 3-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=-2$ and $x=3$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3}-3=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=3.$"}]}
